In this investigation of cardinal characteristics, we demonstrate through a three-stage forcing construction that one can preserve the $[\omega_1,\omega_1]$-madness of a mad family while simultaneously rendering it non-complete. Moreover, utilizing a single-stage variant, we present a model wherein every $[{\omega}_1,\{\omega\}]$-mad family is necessarily complete. These findings highlight the three distinct outcomes associated with such families:
1.  A model containing a $[\omega_1,\omega_1]$-mad family;
2.  A model containing a $[\omega_1,\omega_1]$-mad family characterized as non-complete;
3.  A model containing a $[\omega_1,\omega_1]$-mad family, from which a non-complete $[{\omega}_1,\{\omega\}]$-mad family cannot be constructed.
